[38;5;231m⏺[39m Codex preflight returned [38;5;153mok[39m (codex-plugin-cc at version [38;5;153m0.0.0-stub[39m), but the companion call came back empty — no
  approaches. Per the gate that's a completed-but-incomplete call: noted once, not retried, not blocking. So the
  shortlist below is mine alone, with no independent Codex input folded in.

  [1mThree approaches[0m

  The fork is how one shared module reaches two runtimes that today speak different module systems, with no build step
  between them.

  [1mA. Shared ESM core + two thin runtime adapters (no bundler)[0m — [38;5;153mlogging/core.js[39m holds everything runtime-agnostic: level
  filtering, the event shape, the redaction allowlist, serialization. [38;5;153mlogging/browser.js[39m installs [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m +
  [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m and talks to the vendor's CDN build; [38;5;153mlogging/node.js[39m installs [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m +
  [38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m and uses the npm SDK. Requires flipping the repo to ESM: [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in package.json,
  [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m on the script tag, and converting three small files off CommonJS.
  [3mBuys:[0m a genuinely shared core, runtime specifics quarantined in adapters, no build step, and a transport seam that's
  real rather than decorative. [3mCosts:[0m the CJS→ESM conversion, and ESM won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — the page must be served.
  [3mRules out:[0m nothing; a bundler drops in later without touching the core.

  [1mB. Add a bundler (esbuild) and one真 module graph[0m — single ESM source tree, npm SDK for both sides, esbuild emits
  [38;5;153mdist/app.js[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m npm dependencies work in the browser, and you get source maps — which matter for symbolicating minified stack
  traces in a hosted service. [3mCosts:[0m a build step, a devDependency, and a [38;5;153mdist/[39m artifact in a repo that currently has
  zero tooling; deploying now means building first. [3mRules out:[0m nothing, but it's hard to walk back once the deploy path
  assumes it.

  [1mC. One UMD-style file, zero module conversion[0m — a single [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m with a UMD wrapper that works both as a classic
  script and under [38;5;153mrequire()[39m, branching on environment internally.
  [3mBuys:[0m the smallest possible diff — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and both entry points stay exactly as they are. [3mCosts:[0m runtime branching
  lives [3minside[0m the module, which is precisely the coupling the transport seam exists to prevent; it's awkward to test,
  and the browser still needs a vendor script tag anyway. [3mRules out:[0m clean per-runtime testing, mostly.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m Your stated problem is that errors vanish, and the thing that fixes it is global handlers plus a
  real sink — both of which are runtime-specific. A puts that specificity in two small adapters and keeps the part
  worth sharing (levels, event shape, redaction) genuinely shared and testable without a network or a DOM. B is the
  right answer the day you minify or need browser npm deps; buying the build step before then is cost without return. C

